title: Drill — yes, yes, no, yes, yes
chapter: 14
type: drill
skills: sub-confirm
par: 46
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: Ctrl-H, Replace, Replace, Find Next, Replace, Replace
open: ledger.ts

@brief
Eight times: the parameter `amount` becomes `amountPence`. Four of the
five whole-word hits are code. One is inside a log message a human
reads, and it stays.

Substitute with confirm, answer five times, save. You are drilling the
rhythm of reading a highlighted match and answering it without
breaking stride.

@keys
| `:%s/\<amount\>/amountPence/gc` `Enter` | confirm each whole-word match |
| `y` / `n` | take it / leave it |

@hints
Whole-word anchors keep `REFUND_AMOUNT_INVALID` out of it; the `c`
flag is for the one the anchors cannot see.
--
`:%s/\<amount\>/amountPence/gc`, Enter, then `y y n y y`.

@solution
    :%s/\<amount\>/amountPence/gc<Enter>  y y n y y  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-nine keystrokes, five decisions. By the last rep you should be
reading the line vim shows you, not the count of how many you have
answered. The string is the third hit every time here; in a real file
you will not know where it is until you see it.

@start ledger.ts
export function toLedgerLine(refund: Refund, amount: number) {
  if (amount <= 0) {
    throw exception('REFUND_AMOUNT_INVALID');
  }

  logger.info('refund amount accepted', { refund: refund.id, amount });

  return { refund: refund.id, amount, currency: refund.currency };
}

@expected ledger.ts
export function toLedgerLine(refund: Refund, amountPence: number) {
  if (amountPence <= 0) {
    throw exception('REFUND_AMOUNT_INVALID');
  }

  logger.info('refund amount accepted', { refund: refund.id, amountPence });

  return { refund: refund.id, amountPence, currency: refund.currency };
}
